A pie chart shows part-to-whole proportions as colored wedges in a circle. Use it for budget breakdowns, survey results, resource allocation, and any dataset with a small number of named categories. Uses `pie`.

---

!! Basic example

```
pie title Favourite pets
    "Dogs"  : 45
    "Cats"  : 30
    "Birds" : 15
    "Fish"  : 10
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki.mermaid
pie title Favourite pets
    "Dogs"  : 45
    "Cats"  : 30
    "Birds" : 15
    "Fish"  : 10
$$$

---

!! Real-world example: Engineering team time allocation

This example breaks down how an engineering team allocates time across work categories over a quarter: feature development, bug fixes, technical debt, code review, and meetings. The native Mermaid legend maps each color to its label automatically.

```
pie
    accTitle: Engineering team time allocation Q1
    accDescr: Breakdown of engineering effort by work category

    %% ── Work categories ────────────────────────
    title Engineering Time Allocation — Q1 2024
    "Feature Development"  : 42
    "Bug Fixes"            : 18
    "Technical Debt"       : 15
    "Code Review"          : 14
    "Meetings and Admin"   : 11
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki.mermaid
pie
    accTitle: Engineering team time allocation Q1
    accDescr: Breakdown of engineering effort by work category

    %% ── Work categories ────────────────────────
    title Engineering Time Allocation — Q1 2024
    "Feature Development"  : 42
    "Bug Fixes"            : 18
    "Technical Debt"       : 15
    "Code Review"          : 14
    "Meetings and Admin"   : 11
$$$

---

!! Syntax

```
pie title Optional chart title
    "Label A" : value
    "Label B" : value
    "Label C" : value
```

Values are relative proportions — they do not need to sum to 100. Mermaid normalises them automatically.

!! Tips

* The `pie` keyword renders a built-in color legend automatically — no WikiText legend table is needed.
* Limit to 5–7 slices; too many small slices become hard to read. Combine minor items into an "Other" category.
* Values can be integers or decimals (`42`, `18.5`).
* `pie showData` displays the raw values inside or near each slice (optional keyword).
* See also: [[XY Chart]] for bar/line data, [[Quadrant Chart]] for positioning by two axes.

